## Connections

A connection object is a wrapper around an MQTT client and provides specific functionality to support both clients and servers.
Generally, you only need one connection object per daemon/program, as it can support multiple clients and servers.  
For most languages, Stinger-IPC does not require a specific connection object implementation, as long as it implements the required interface.

The application code is responsible for creating and managing the connection object, including connecting to the MQTT broker.

## Discovery

Because there may be multiple instances of the same Stinger Interface, a discovery mechanism is provided to find and connect to them.  A discovery class is provided which connects to
the MQTT broker and listens for Stinger Interface announcements.  The discovery class can then provide client instances.

A server publishes its announcement as a **retained** message when it starts up, and re-publishes it every {{config.discovery.advert_interval_seconds}} seconds to keep the retained value fresh.  Because the message is retained, a discovery class that starts listening after a server is already running still receives its current announcement right away.  On a clean shutdown, the server publishes an empty payload to the same topic, which tells any listening discovery class that the instance is going away.

```plantuml
@startuml
box Consumer
participant Client
participant Discovery
end box
participant Broker
box Producer
participant Server
end box
Server -> Broker : PUB Announcement(ServiceInfo)\n[retained]
Discovery -> Broker : SUB Announcement Topic
Discovery <- Broker : PUB Annoucement(ServiceInfo)
Discovery -> Client ** : ServiceInfo
Client -> Broker : SUB all the things
Client -> Client : Do all the things
@enduml
```

#### Announcement Contents

| Name | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| instance | string | Identifies the specific instance of the service. |
| {{config.topics.params|join(", ")}} | string | Topic parameter(s) that identify this instance on the broker. |
| methods | map&lt;string,string&gt; | Maps method names to their version string, for methods that declare a version. |
| commands | map&lt;string,string&gt; | Maps command names to their version string, for commands that declare a version. |

#### MQTT Details

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Topic | `{{stinger.interface_info_topic()}}` |
| QoS | 1 |
| Retain | true |

| Property | Value |
|---|---|
| content-type | `application/json` |

## Server

A server is a _provider_ of functionality.  It {%if stinger.signals|length > 0%}sends signals {%endif%}{%if stinger.methods|length > 0 %}and handles method calls {%endif%}{%if stinger.commands|length > 0 %}and receives commands {%endif%}{%if stinger.properties|length > 0 %}and owns property values{%endif%}.

When constructing a server instance, a connection object{%if stinger.properties | length > 0 %} and initial property values must be provided{%endif%}.

## Client

A client is a _utilizer_ of functionality.  It receives signals, makes method calls, sends commands, reads property values, or requests updates to property values.

A client does not subscribe to a property until the application first asks for it.
Registering a changed-value handler subscribes, so the handler is called with the
server's current value shortly afterwards.  Reading a property also subscribes, then
waits up to five seconds for the value and fails if none arrives.  Writing a property
does the same{%if config.properties.version_tracking%}, because the update request has to carry the version of the value the
client last saw{%endif%}.


{%if stinger.signals | length > 0 %}
## Signals

Signals are messages from a server to clients.

A client subscribes to a signal only when the application first registers a handler for
it (or, in Rust, asks for its receiver), so a signal nothing is listening for is never
received.  Signals are not retained, so a signal emitted before a handler is registered
is missed.

```plantuml
@startuml
Client <<- Server : Signal(Parameters)
@enduml
```
